  "parent_closes": false,
  "parent_reason": "Neither gap establishes the eventual estimate required by the criterion. Conditional on both gaps, the arithmetic and numerical deductions work. Write k = 9240q, so M = 2k and a = k - 1. Any common divisor of a and M divides 2, and a is odd, so a is a unit. Also 8 divides k, giving d ≡ 7 mod 8. Since d + 1 ≡ k mod 2k, M divides 2(d + 1) and does not divide d + 1. For r = Re ρ > 1/2, the chosen ε satisfies 0 < ε ≤ 1/102 < 1/100, and β = 1/2 + (8/51)(min(r,1) - 1/2) lies strictly between 1/2 and r. For each fixed prime d in the class, division by the positive mAH and substitution of the Gap 2 bounds would give R_M(x) ≤ φ(M)x^(6ε-1/2) + x^(-1000000) for all sufficiently large x. Dividing this upper bound by x^(β-1) gives φ(M)x^(-2ε) + x^(-1000000-8ε+1/2), which tends to zero. The threshold may depend on d, as the criterion permits. The criterion would then apply to the induced character modulo M. Reduction on unit groups is surjective, by the Chinese remainder theorem, so induction preserves principality. On the usual pole-removal interpretation of the entire L-function, regularization preserves zeros at every point under consideration, since its extra factor in the principal case is ρ - 1 and that case excludes ρ = 1. The finite Euler factors for changing modulus then give the claimed nonvanishing."
